# Scheduled jobs for a non-container deployment (cron). Install with: crontab deploy/crontab.example
#
# None of these four is optional. Without the first, exam attempts abandoned mid-way stay
# in_progress forever and are never scored. Without the second, invitation emails are never sent
# AT ALL - creating an Invitation only queues it (email_status=QUEUED); process_email_queue is
# the only thing that actually sends one. This is not a backup job the way it used to be (see
# the command's own module docstring) - it is the entire invitation-sending pipeline, which is
# why it runs every minute rather than every 15. Without the third, a candidate whose browser or
# Safe Exam Browser closes mid-exam is never flagged as such - the attempt just sits in_progress
# until it eventually times out on its own, indistinguishable from someone who ran to the deadline
# on purpose. Without the fourth, a TA whose browser/network won't handle a session recording's
# native WebM never gets an MP4 copy of it.
#
# Draft batches are NOT auto-deleted - that 24-hour expiry was removed. A Draft now sits until a
# TA/admin deletes it explicitly from the Drafts list (BatchList.jsx).

SHELL=/bin/sh
# Adjust to the deployment's paths.
APP=/srv/talentgate/Backend
PY=/srv/talentgate/Backend/venv/bin/python

# Finalize exam attempts past their deadline.
*/10 * * * * cd $APP && $PY manage.py finalize_expired_attempts >> /var/log/talentgate/scheduler.log 2>&1

# Send every queued invitation email. Every minute, not less often - a candidate is waiting on
# this for their assessment link, not just cleanup. flock guards against a run that is still
# working through a large batch (up to --max 200 rows, paced by INVITE_SEND_DELAY_SECONDS -
# minutes, not seconds, for a big batch) still being alive when the next minute's tick fires;
# without it, cron would overlap two runs and risk sending the same invitation twice. -n makes a
# tick that finds the lock held simply skip rather than queue up behind it - the next tick a
# minute later is a better use of that time than a growing backlog of waiting cron processes.
* * * * * flock -n /tmp/talentgate-process-email-queue.lock $PY $APP/manage.py process_email_queue >> /var/log/talentgate/scheduler.log 2>&1

# Catch a browser/Safe Exam Browser closing mid-exam. Every 30 seconds (cron's own finest grain
# is one minute, so this uses the same sleep-loop shape as startup.sh rather than a second crontab
# line) - the whole point of this command is noticing soon after it happens, not on some later
# housekeeping sweep. Safe to run concurrently with itself if a run is still going when the next
# would start - see the command's own module docstring on why no flock is needed here.
* * * * * cd $APP && for i in 0 30; do (sleep $i; $PY manage.py terminate_stale_attempts >> /var/log/talentgate/scheduler.log 2>&1) & done; wait

# Convert finished attempts' session recordings to MP4. No candidate or TA is waiting on this in
# real time the way they are for the jobs above - it just bounds how long after an exam ends
# before its MP4 copy shows up on Candidate Details.
*/10 * * * * cd $APP && $PY manage.py transcode_recordings >> /var/log/talentgate/scheduler.log 2>&1
